#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111735} :activity/id #uuid "ec05fcce-5a67-533c-840a-322806556dbc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050714 :topic/id #uuid "60b97f00-f86a-5a4f-834e-1a83f8ee3cc2" :topic/math-academy-id 1453} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111735 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111872 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $P$ is a point in the first quadrant that lies on the unit circle. We can then draw a right triangle, where the hypotenuse is the line segment between the origin and the point. \n\n![](images/4b/4bae3a49b5557df9e5b50280d744aa72048b2d224df042e103a6b5d156467de7.png)\n\nSince the hypotenuse is the radius of the unit circle, its length must be equal to $1.$ And, since the triangle is a right triangle, the $(x,y)$ coordinates of any point on the unit circle in the first quadrant must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2=1.\n$$\n\nWe can use this to find the $y$-coordinate of a point given the $x$-coordinate, and vice-versa.\n\nFor example, suppose that the $x$-coordinate of a point $P$ in the first quadrant on the unit circle is $\\dfrac{3}{7}.$ To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x=\\dfrac{3}{7}$ into the above and solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tx^2+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t          \\left(\\dfrac{3}{7}\\right)^2+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{9}{49}+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t\ty^2&= 1-\\dfrac{9}{49}\\[5pt]\n\t\ty^2&= \\dfrac{40}{49}\\[5pt]\n\t\ty&= \\pm\\sqrt{\\dfrac{40}{49}}\\[5pt]\n\t        y &= \\pm \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}\n\n}\n$$\n\nSince $P$ lies in the first quadrant, $y$ must be positive. So we select the positive answer. \n\nTherefore, our final answer is $y=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b4029eb2-0222-5d8b-a285-7f0089655736" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2718 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "91d91920-7585-54d5-a3bb-aa4f02c97c17" :step/math-academy-id 87629 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111736}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111736 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110754 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110456 :question/id #uuid "42313322-7535-5969-ad84-958449e48c1b" :question/math-academy-id "e-3480"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87c18ea8-af12-52bd-8420-ec24ed63de65" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049224}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510724} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335778}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Pythagorean Theorem on the Unit Circle"} :step/id #uuid "51014196-5277-5ae7-8d38-8cacaa66b4eb" :step/math-academy-id 87630 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111737}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111737 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111867 :tutorial/content "Recall that the coordinates of a point on the unit circle with a central angle $\\theta$ have the property\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf this point is in the first quadrant, we can substitute these values into the Pythagorean Identity from before to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last expression is known as the **Pythagorean trigonometric identity,** and is arguably the most important equation in trigonometry.\n\nThe Pythagorean trigonometric identity holds for *every* angle $\\theta,$ not just if $\\theta$ is in the first quadrant. In this lesson, we're focusing on the first quadrant only. We'll learn how to deal with angles in other quadrants in a future lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "f0d65e8f-ee17-505a-8a0c-a8b8c87f02e2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2550 :tutorial/title "The Pythagorean Identity"} :step/id #uuid "13bf9525-3d5e-5ef0-b342-b104b3a91ae7" :step/math-academy-id 87633 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111738}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111738 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110755 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110457 :question/id #uuid "02c747c2-0eda-5c53-8e43-829f878dd278" :question/math-academy-id "e-3482"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c73db44-2ec2-5025-9431-acb02e414916" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050291}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511715} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335735} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511708}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"} :step/id #uuid "e5d4c5eb-0cce-5c47-afdd-6deeebc20fba" :step/math-academy-id 87634 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111739}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111739 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110756 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110458 :question/id #uuid "bbf71023-510c-54af-9d0c-4df6744e3007" :question/math-academy-id "e-3484"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "435bc6f9-651f-511c-a972-9afe90baff32" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051029}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511716} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186